in{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420790 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420790 #edb/ref 1069 "q-138579" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420790 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420790 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{U}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}5\\end{aligned}],\\,{V}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420790 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "83e058d9-31bd-5eef-83c8-b6f7c1427845" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420790 #edb/ref 1074 "If $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${Σ}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{Σ}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420791 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420791 #edb/ref 1069 "q-138620" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420791 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420791 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{U}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}],\\,{V}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420791 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a18b0561-3c55-5481-a95c-144e99d62fb8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420791 #edb/ref 1074 "If $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${Σ}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{Σ}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420792 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420792 #edb/ref 1069 "q-138658" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420792 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420792 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{U}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}],\\,{V}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420792 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f2315875-6b0f-5492-8e9e-75741c68b770" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420792 #edb/ref 1074 "If $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${Σ}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420793 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420793 #edb/ref 1069 "q-138535" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420793 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420793 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & 0 & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420793 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f7a5359f-8967-570a-b634-daaee8dbc9c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420793 #edb/ref 1074 "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of $Σ.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}^{+}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{10} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned